    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d647">

                <pb facs="0874" n="862" sameAs="frus:frus1945v05/pg862"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EW/6–2745: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-06-27T21:00:00-04:00">June 27, 1945—9
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1440. For Harriman from Harry L. Hopkins: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I talked with President Truman about Eisenhower’s<anchor n="6"
                                xml:id="d647-f1"/> visit to Moscow. He was quite enthusiastic about
                            it. Eisenhower is taking a rest and I doubt very much if it could be
                            worked out prior to the Berlin conference<anchor n="7" xml:id="d647-f2"
                            /> but it might well be done immediately thereafter. The President told
                            me he would like to ask Zhukov<anchor n="8" xml:id="d647-f3"/> to come
                            to the United States at about the same time. I am taking this up with
                            General Marshall.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d647-f4"/>
                        </p>
                        <pb facs="0875" n="863" xml:id="pg863"/>
                        <p>I want to send you my warmest congratulations on the negotiations in
                                Moscow.<anchor n="10" xml:id="d647-f5"/> Apart from the Hearst and
                            McCormick papers they have received a warm welcome from the American
                            people.</p>
                        <p>Louie arrived this morning from France.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d647-f1" n="6" xml:id="d647-f1-content">
                <p>General of the Army Dwight D. Eisenhower, Supreme Commander, Allied Expeditionary
                    Force in Europe.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d647-f2" n="7" xml:id="d647-f2-content">
                <p>For documentation on the Conference, held July 16–August 2, 1945, see <ref
                        target="frus1945Berlinv01">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Conference of Berlin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Potsdam</hi>), 2 vols</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d647-f3" n="8" xml:id="d647-f3-content">
                <p>Marshal of the Soviet Union Georgy Konstantinovich Zhukov, Chief, Soviet Military
                    Administration in Germany, and Commander-in-Chief of Soviet Forces of Occupation
                    in Germany.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d647-f4" n="9" xml:id="d647-f4-content">
                <p>General of the Army George C. Marshall, Chief of Staff, United States Army.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d647-f5" n="10" xml:id="d647-f5-content">
                <p>The negotiations referred to were those of the Polish Commission for the
                    formation of a Provisional Polish Government of National Unity; for
                    documentation, see <ref target="#pg110">pp. 110</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
